1
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:06,360
 Welcome to MemodoAI.

2
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:08,360
 In the next 20 minutes or so,

3
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:10,100
 we'll walk through everything you need

4
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:11,770
 to be productive on your first day.

5
00:00:11,770 --> 00:00:14,880
 The basics, the few features that surprise people,

6
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:17,130
 and the workflows that actually pay off

7
00:00:17,130 --> 00:00:18,760
 once you've used it for a week.

8
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:22,430
 MemodoAI is Memodo's internal AI workspace.

9
00:00:22,430 --> 00:00:25,550
 If you've used ChatGPT or Claude before,

10
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:26,660
 this will feel familiar.

11
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:30,590
 The main difference, it runs on infrastructure we control,

12
00:00:30,590 --> 00:00:34,010
 so internal data stays inside Memodo's environment.

13
00:00:34,010 --> 00:00:39,590
 You can use it for the obvious things, drafting emails, summarizing documents,

14
00:00:39,590 --> 00:00:45,720
 explaining unfamiliar concepts, polishing rough notes, and for a few less obvious

15
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:51,540
 things, chatting with your own files, building small agents tailored to a recurring

16
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:55,840
 task, and rendering interactive artifacts inline in the chat.

17
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:59,180
 Two ground rules before we dive in.

18
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:03,540
 MemodoAI is great at being a fast-first drafter.

19
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:05,350
 It is not a source of truth.

20
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:11,570
 always check facts that matter and when you're working with sensitive data the

21
00:01:11,570 --> 00:01:12,160
 principle of

22
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:17,560
 use the minimum you need still applies we'll come back to this when we talk about PII

23
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:18,640
 warnings later

24
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:29,560
 the interface has three regions left to right on the far left is a column of icons

25
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:30,520
 your control

26
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:37,180
 panel from top to bottom open or close the sidebar start a new chat your chat

27
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:37,900
 history

28
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:44,960
 The agent builder, prompts, memories, bookmarks, attach files, parameters, and at

29
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:46,920
 the very bottom, your account avatar.

30
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:49,670
 Hover over any of them to see its name.

31
00:01:49,670 --> 00:01:54,960
 To the right of those icons is the conversation sidebar, where your past chats are

32
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:56,370
 grouped by recency.

33
00:01:56,370 --> 00:02:00,060
 And the big middle column is where you actually work.

34
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:03,780
 The top bar shows the active model or agent.

35
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:06,800
 The composer at the bottom is where you type.

36
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:10,780
 The Composer has four buttons that shift its behavior.

37
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:16,240
 A paperclip for attaching files, a sliders icon for tweaking parameters,

38
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:21,230
 a search button for the web, and a file search button for chatting with documents.

39
00:02:21,230 --> 00:02:23,500
 We'll touch each of these in turn.

40
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:29,800
 Let's send our first message.

41
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:35,030
 The default model is GPT-5, and unless you have a reason to change it,

42
00:02:35,030 --> 00:02:37,860
 that's the right choice for almost everything.

43
00:02:37,860 --> 00:02:42,790
 I'll type a question, something you might actually ask on a workday.

44
00:02:42,790 --> 00:02:46,860
 What's the difference between OKRs and KPIs?

45
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:50,430
 Give me a clear explanation I can share with my team.

46
00:02:50,430 --> 00:02:52,770
 I'll send that.

47
00:02:52,770 --> 00:02:56,570
 Notice the conversation auto-titles itself once the model finishes.

48
00:02:56,570 --> 00:02:59,240
 It picks something descriptive based on the content.

49
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:03,560
 You can always rename it manually by hovering over the title in the sidebar.

50
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:07,910
 Now, under each assistant response, you'll see a row of icons.

51
00:03:07,910 --> 00:03:13,490
 From left to right, read aloud, copy, edit, fork,

52
00:03:13,490 --> 00:03:17,040
 which means continue from this point in a new branch of the conversation.

53
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:24,080
 thumbs up, thumbs down, and regenerate. One quick tip while we're here. If you don't

54
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:24,820
 like a response,

55
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:29,190
 it's almost always better to edit your original message and resend than to argue

56
00:03:29,190 --> 00:03:30,070
 with the model

57
00:03:30,070 --> 00:03:34,800
 in a follow-up. Click the pencil icon on your own message, refine the question, and

58
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:35,480
 you'll get a

59
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:45,320
 cleaner answer in fewer turns. Same conversation, less noise. Click the model name

60
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:46,240
 in the top bar

61
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:51,270
 to open the picker. You'll see two groups. The first is MemodoAI. That's the base

62
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:52,120
 models available

63
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:58,760
 in your instance. Right now, that's GPT-5 with document upload support. The second

64
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:00,050
 is my agents.

65
00:04:00,050 --> 00:04:04,970
 These are pre-configured assistants that you or a colleague have built. Picking one

66
00:04:04,970 --> 00:04:05,560
 of these from

67
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:10,490
 the model picker is the fastest way to start a chat with that agent. The rule of

68
00:04:10,490 --> 00:04:11,400
 thumb for which

69
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:16,730
 to pick. Leave it on GPT-5 unless you're doing something an agent was specifically

70
00:04:16,730 --> 00:04:17,570
 built for.

71
00:04:17,570 --> 00:04:26,720
 There are two things in MemodoAI that look similar but solve different problems,

72
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:28,730
 presets and agents.

73
00:04:28,730 --> 00:04:34,880
 Presets are saved bundles of model parameters, things like temperature, max tokens,

74
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:36,150
 frequency penalty.

75
00:04:36,150 --> 00:04:40,200
 They live behind the Presets button, next to the model picker.

76
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:45,070
 The defaults that MemodoAI ships with have been chosen for sensible behavior.

77
00:04:45,070 --> 00:04:49,260
 Unless you have a specific reason to tweak them, leave them alone.

78
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:54,140
 Changing temperature without a clear hypothesis usually makes the model worse, not

79
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:54,860
 better.

80
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:58,670
 Agents, on the other hand, are full personas.

81
00:04:58,670 --> 00:05:04,200
 They have a name, an icon, a system prompt that defines how they behave, and

82
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:05,620
 optional tools.

83
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:09,590
 Use an agent when the same role keeps coming up.

84
00:05:09,590 --> 00:05:11,620
 We'll build one in a few minutes.

85
00:05:11,620 --> 00:05:17,480
 The short version, presets, are an advanced power user feature.

86
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:19,230
 Ignore them if you're new.

87
00:05:19,810 --> 00:05:24,560
 Agents are what you'll actually use day to day.

88
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:33,620
 That dotted icon in the top right corner toggles temporary chat mode.

89
00:05:33,620 --> 00:05:40,270
 When it's on, the composer gets a purple ring, and the conversation is not saved to

90
00:05:40,270 --> 00:05:40,870
 your history.

91
00:05:40,870 --> 00:05:45,660
 Use this for one-off questions you don't want sitting in your sidebar.

92
00:05:45,660 --> 00:05:50,860
 Quick definitions, throwaway brainstorms, sensitive drafts.

93
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:53,310
 Closing the tab also ends it.

94
00:05:53,310 --> 00:05:57,670
 Anything you send in temporary chat is gone the moment the chat closes.

95
00:05:57,670 --> 00:06:06,490
 The model is trained on data that's months old by the time you're using it.

96
00:06:06,490 --> 00:06:10,140
 To pull in fresh information, toggle the search button on.

97
00:06:10,140 --> 00:06:13,510
 It's the globe icon in the composer.

98
00:06:13,510 --> 00:06:16,680
 I'll ask a question that needs current data.

99
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:21,060
 The model decomposes the question into multiple web searches automatically.

100
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:25,010
 You'll see a Used N tools indicator at the top of the response.

101
00:06:25,010 --> 00:06:28,880
 Click it to see exactly what the model searched for and what it found.

102
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:35,100
 Search is great for anything time-sensitive. Pricing, news, current versions,

103
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:36,210
 recent events.

104
00:06:36,210 --> 00:06:41,080
 It struggles with very specific local data, niche corporate information, and

105
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:42,060
 anything behind a

106
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:47,320
 paywall. If a search comes back unclear, the model will usually offer to refine,

107
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:48,590
 guide it,

108
00:06:48,590 --> 00:06:59,580
 name a source, narrow the scope, or specify what kind of answer you want. This is

109
00:06:59,580 --> 00:07:00,270
 one of the most

110
00:07:00,270 --> 00:07:02,890
 useful features in MemodoAI.

111
00:07:02,890 --> 00:07:06,050
 You attach a document, ask questions about it,

112
00:07:06,050 --> 00:07:09,680
 and the model answers with citations back to the source.

113
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:12,700
 Step one, turn on file search.

114
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:14,940
 Click the toggle in the composer.

115
00:07:14,940 --> 00:07:19,530
 Without this, the paperclip only lets you upload images.

116
00:07:19,530 --> 00:07:22,780
 Step two, attach the document.

117
00:07:22,780 --> 00:07:24,210
 Click the paperclip.

118
00:07:24,210 --> 00:07:26,200
 Now you have two options.

119
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:28,480
 Pick upload document.

120
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:30,930
 MemodoAI supports the formats you'd expect

121
00:07:30,930 --> 00:07:32,870
 from a modern office workflow.

122
00:07:32,870 --> 00:07:37,870
 PDFs, Word documents, markdown, plain text, CSVs,

123
00:07:37,870 --> 00:07:40,210
 spreadsheets, slide decks,

124
00:07:40,210 --> 00:07:42,530
 and most common source code formats.

125
00:07:42,530 --> 00:07:45,830
 There's a size limit around 25 megabytes per file.

126
00:07:45,830 --> 00:07:48,870
 If a file gets rejected, split it first.

127
00:07:48,870 --> 00:07:51,560
 Step three, ask.

128
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:55,520
 I'm going to ask, what are the three Q3 priorities

129
00:07:55,520 --> 00:07:57,120
 and who owns each one?

130
00:07:57,120 --> 00:07:58,690
 Cite the document.

131
00:07:58,690 --> 00:08:00,250
 Notice the response.

132
00:08:00,250 --> 00:08:02,430
 Each claim is tied back to the source.

133
00:08:02,430 --> 00:08:03,620
 That's the citation behavior.

134
00:08:03,620 --> 00:08:08,660
 And the Searched Your Files expander shows the actual chunks of the document the

135
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:09,200
 model read.

136
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:12,030
 You can verify any claim by clicking through.

137
00:08:12,030 --> 00:08:13,390
 Two things to know.

138
00:08:13,390 --> 00:08:17,120
 One, you can attach multiple documents to one chat.

139
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:19,580
 The model will search across all of them.

140
00:08:19,580 --> 00:08:24,210
 Two, for long documents, ask focused questions.

141
00:08:24,210 --> 00:08:31,400
 Who owns priority two is much better than tell me about this doc.

142
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:35,620
 The narrower your question, the better the citation behavior.

143
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:44,150
 Agents are pre-configured assistants, someone built for a specific job.

144
00:08:44,150 --> 00:08:49,620
 They have a name, an icon, a system prompt, and optionally, some tools wired up

145
00:08:49,620 --> 00:08:51,470
 like file search or web search.

146
00:08:51,470 --> 00:08:53,940
 Let's start by using a shared agent.

147
00:08:53,940 --> 00:08:58,600
 From the model picker, I'll pick Memodo Engineering Concierge.

148
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:04,080
 The chat area changes to show the agent's landing page, its name, its icon, and a

149
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:06,620
 short description of what it's for.

150
00:09:07,550 --> 00:09:11,430
 When I send a message, the agent uses its tools to find an answer.

151
00:09:11,430 --> 00:09:16,160
 Notice the Used N tools line at the top of the response.

152
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:20,640
 Same idea as web search, just with the agent's own tools.

153
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:25,070
 Agents that have been built by other people, like this one, are read-only.

154
00:09:25,070 --> 00:09:28,190
 You can use them, but not edit their instructions.

155
00:09:28,190 --> 00:09:30,460
 Now, let's build our own.

156
00:09:30,460 --> 00:09:34,740
 I'll open the Agent Builder. That's the robot icon in the left rail.

157
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:38,440
 If I had a read-only agent selected, I'd see a

158
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:40,980
 You don't have access to edit this agent message.

159
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:44,740
 I'll click Create New Agent at the top.

160
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:49,430
 The blank form has fields for the name, a short description, the category, the

161
00:09:49,430 --> 00:09:50,250
 instructions,

162
00:09:50,250 --> 00:09:53,850
 which is the system prompt, the model, and the capabilities.

163
00:09:53,850 --> 00:09:57,340
 The instructions are the heart of an agent.

164
00:09:57,340 --> 00:09:59,020
 Be specific.

165
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:05,820
 What input it expects, what output it should produce, what tone to use, and what it

166
00:10:05,820 --> 00:10:06,740
 must not do.

167
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:10,670
 For our example, I'll create a meeting notes polisher.

168
00:10:10,670 --> 00:10:15,450
 It takes rough bullet point notes and produces clean, structured minutes.

169
00:10:15,450 --> 00:10:20,040
 The system prompt tells it exactly what sections to include.

170
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:25,420
 Attendees, key decisions, action items, discussion summary, open questions.

171
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:28,570
 It tells it to mark missing fields as TBD.

172
00:10:28,570 --> 00:10:33,370
 And critically, it forbids inventing facts that aren't in the source.

173
00:10:33,370 --> 00:10:36,320
 I'll pick GPT-5 as the model.

174
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:38,810
 Azure OpenAI as the provider.

175
00:10:38,810 --> 00:10:40,920
 Click Create.

176
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:43,000
 Now I can use it.

177
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:47,370
 I'll start a new chat with the meeting notes polisher and paste in some rough notes.

178
00:10:47,370 --> 00:10:50,470
 The agent follows its instructions exactly.

179
00:10:50,470 --> 00:10:54,740
 Structured sections, owners, and deadlines.

180
00:10:54,740 --> 00:10:56,040
 Extracted.

181
00:10:56,040 --> 00:10:58,580
 TBD, where the source didn't specify.

182
00:10:58,580 --> 00:11:00,630
 No invented facts.

183
00:11:00,630 --> 00:11:04,090
 Three rules for writing good agent instructions, by the way.

184
00:11:04,090 --> 00:11:07,900
 One, describe input and output explicitly.

185
00:11:07,900 --> 00:11:11,320
 The model follows shape more reliably than vibes.

186
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:14,110
 Two, forbid the failure modes.

187
00:11:14,110 --> 00:11:17,850
 never invent facts not in the source

188
00:11:17,850 --> 00:11:20,580
 is more important than describing what right looks like.

189
00:11:20,580 --> 00:11:23,150
 Three, set the tone in one sentence.

190
00:11:23,150 --> 00:11:25,880
 The model will hold the tone if you tell it once.

191
00:11:25,880 --> 00:11:33,040
 There's one more agent capability worth showing, artifacts.

192
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:36,550
 When you ask MemodoAI for something visual,

193
00:11:36,550 --> 00:11:39,350
 a chart, a small UI mock-up,

194
00:11:39,350 --> 00:11:41,940
 a styled table, an interactive widget,

195
00:11:41,940 --> 00:11:44,550
 the result can be rendered as an artifact,

196
00:11:44,550 --> 00:11:47,880
 a self-contained interactive panel

197
00:11:47,880 --> 00:11:51,320
 that appears next to the chat instead of as a plain code block.

198
00:11:51,320 --> 00:11:54,550
 To use it, the agent needs artifacts enabled.

199
00:11:54,550 --> 00:11:58,150
 In the Agent Builder, scroll the Capabilities section.

200
00:11:58,150 --> 00:12:04,780
 You'll see two toggles, Enable Artifacts and Include shadcn/ui components instructions.

201
00:12:04,780 --> 00:12:09,590
 The second toggle tells the agent about a popular component library, so its

202
00:12:09,590 --> 00:12:11,030
 artifacts look cleaner.

203
00:12:11,030 --> 00:12:13,980
 Once enabled, ask for something visual.

204
00:12:15,180 --> 00:12:18,810
 I'm going to ask the Meeting Notes Polisher to render the action items as an

205
00:12:18,810 --> 00:12:20,870
 interactive HTML checklist

206
00:12:20,870 --> 00:12:25,130
 with checkboxes, owner labels, and a progress counter at the top.

207
00:12:25,130 --> 00:12:29,490
 There it is, a side panel artifact, fully interactive.

208
00:12:29,490 --> 00:12:33,290
 I can check items off, the progress counter updates,

209
00:12:33,290 --> 00:12:36,930
 and I can switch between the rendered preview and the underlying code with the

210
00:12:36,930 --> 00:12:37,960
 toggle at the top.

211
00:12:37,960 --> 00:12:42,280
 Artifacts are best for one-off prototypes and visual answers.

212
00:12:42,280 --> 00:12:45,610
 The state inside an artifact isn't saved.

213
00:12:46,370 --> 00:12:48,880
 If you check items off, that's just for the current view.

214
00:12:48,880 --> 00:12:57,210
 MemodoAI remembers context across chats, so you don't have to keep reintroducing

215
00:12:57,210 --> 00:12:57,840
 yourself.

216
00:12:57,840 --> 00:13:03,110
 Things like your name, your role, ongoing projects you mentioned, recurring people

217
00:13:03,110 --> 00:13:03,820
 you work with.

218
00:13:03,820 --> 00:13:06,090
 These get quietly saved.

219
00:13:06,090 --> 00:13:09,620
 Click the brain icon in the left rail to see what's saved.

220
00:13:09,620 --> 00:13:16,110
 Each memory has a key, a short identifier, a value, the actual content, a token

221
00:13:16,110 --> 00:13:18,290
 count, and a date.

222
00:13:18,920 --> 00:13:23,270
 You can edit any of them by clicking the pencil icon or delete with the trash icon.

223
00:13:23,270 --> 00:13:28,220
 The Use Memory toggle at the top of the panel controls whether the model has access

224
00:13:28,220 --> 00:13:29,630
 to memories during your chats.

225
00:13:29,630 --> 00:13:33,390
 Turn it off if you want the model to behave like it's never met you.

226
00:13:33,390 --> 00:13:35,510
 Useful for testing prompts.

227
00:13:35,510 --> 00:13:37,130
 Two things to know.

228
00:13:37,130 --> 00:13:39,820
 Memories are tied to your account only.

229
00:13:39,820 --> 00:13:41,620
 Nobody else can see them.

230
00:13:41,620 --> 00:13:45,240
 And anything you don't want stored, you can delete in one click.

231
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:47,800
 The trash icon is fast.

232
00:13:52,350 --> 00:13:56,920
 The prompts panel, that's the pencil icon in the left rail, is a personal library

233
00:13:56,920 --> 00:14:00,790
 of reusable prompts, things you find yourself typing repeatedly.

234
00:14:00,790 --> 00:14:03,170
 Click the plus to create one.

235
00:14:03,170 --> 00:14:08,520
 You give it a name, optionally a category, the prompt text itself, and optionally a

236
00:14:08,520 --> 00:14:10,760
 slash command that triggers it.

237
00:14:10,760 --> 00:14:16,890
 With send prompts on select, checked at the top of the panel, clicking a saved

238
00:14:16,890 --> 00:14:18,980
 prompt sends it immediately.

239
00:14:18,980 --> 00:14:23,850
 The rule of thumb, a prompt is a one-shot instruction.

240
00:14:25,190 --> 00:14:27,480
 An agent is an ongoing persona.

241
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:31,180
 If you find yourself selecting the same prompt every day,

242
00:14:31,180 --> 00:14:34,190
 that's a sign it should be promoted to an agent instead.

243
00:14:34,190 --> 00:14:41,490
 Bookmarks are tags you can apply to conversations to find them later.

244
00:14:41,490 --> 00:14:44,080
 Useful once you have a long chat history.

245
00:14:44,080 --> 00:14:45,880
 Open any conversation.

246
00:14:45,880 --> 00:14:49,900
 Click the bookmark icon in the top bar, then New Bookmark.

247
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:55,400
 Give it a title, typically a category like Project X or Reference,

248
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:57,560
 and optionally a description.

249
00:14:58,580 --> 00:15:03,380
 The Add to Current Conversation checkbox tags this chat with the bookmark on the

250
00:15:03,380 --> 00:15:03,910
 spot.

251
00:15:03,910 --> 00:15:13,200
 One more thing worth understanding before you start using MemodoAI for real work.

252
00:15:13,200 --> 00:15:15,150
 The PII warning.

253
00:15:15,150 --> 00:15:21,100
 MemodoAI scans your messages for things that look like personal or sensitive data.

254
00:15:21,100 --> 00:15:27,180
 Credit card numbers, social security numbers, email addresses, phone numbers,

255
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:29,680
 addresses, financial identifiers.

256
00:15:30,570 --> 00:15:33,920
 If the detector finds something, you'll see an orange banner at the top of the

257
00:15:33,920 --> 00:15:35,020
 screen calling it out.

258
00:15:35,020 --> 00:15:38,440
 I'll demonstrate with a clearly fake test prompt.

259
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:40,880
 Notice when I send this.

260
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:42,660
 There's the banner.

261
00:15:42,660 --> 00:15:46,180
 Your message appears to contain personal information.

262
00:15:46,180 --> 00:15:49,650
 It identifies the categories that were flagged.

263
00:15:49,650 --> 00:15:52,170
 A few important things to know.

264
00:15:52,170 --> 00:15:56,840
 One, this is a heads-up, not a hard stop.

265
00:15:56,840 --> 00:15:59,660
 The message still goes to the model.

266
00:16:00,320 --> 00:16:03,110
 So if you notice mid-send that you shouldn't have included something,

267
00:16:03,110 --> 00:16:06,920
 use the stop generating button in the composer to halt the response,

268
00:16:06,920 --> 00:16:09,200
 then delete the conversation if needed.

269
00:16:09,200 --> 00:16:12,990
 Two, the detector isn't perfect.

270
00:16:12,990 --> 00:16:17,970
 It can flag things that aren't really PII and it can miss things that are.

271
00:16:17,970 --> 00:16:21,090
 Treat it as a safety net, not a checkpoint.

272
00:16:21,090 --> 00:16:23,990
 You're still responsible for what you send.

273
00:16:23,990 --> 00:16:27,560
 Three, under the hood.

274
00:16:27,560 --> 00:16:31,890
 This is powered by Memodo's PII detection service, redakt.

275
00:16:31,890 --> 00:16:34,490
 Compliance can adjust its sensitivity.

276
00:16:35,350 --> 00:16:39,800
 The simple version, when the banner appears, stop and read it.

277
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:43,950
 Most of the time, you'll want to edit the message and resend with placeholders

278
00:16:43,950 --> 00:16:45,240
 instead of real data.

279
00:16:45,240 --> 00:16:53,330
 A few quick principles before we wrap up.

280
00:16:53,330 --> 00:16:54,730
 Be specific.

281
00:16:54,730 --> 00:16:57,750
 Make this professional is vague.

282
00:16:57,750 --> 00:17:01,290
 Rewrite this email so it sounds direct but warm.

283
00:17:01,290 --> 00:17:04,740
 Three short paragraphs, no greeting, is actionable.

284
00:17:04,740 --> 00:17:07,460
 The model follows shape better than vibes.

285
00:17:07,460 --> 00:17:10,140
 Edit before you re-ask.

286
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:15,710
 Re-sending a slightly clearer prompt almost always beats trying to steer the model

287
00:17:15,710 --> 00:17:17,210
 through a long follow-up.

288
00:17:17,210 --> 00:17:20,990
 The model can be wrong. Always check facts that matter.

289
00:17:20,990 --> 00:17:25,040
 The model is a very fast drafter, not an oracle.

290
00:17:25,040 --> 00:17:28,490
 Sensitive data. Stick to the minimum you need.

291
00:17:28,490 --> 00:17:33,550
 If a task doesn't need a real name or a real number, redact first.

292
00:17:33,550 --> 00:17:38,140
 The PII warning is a safety net, not a replacement for thinking.

293
00:17:38,140 --> 00:17:40,760
 That's the tour.

294
00:17:42,310 --> 00:17:45,380
 Try a few things and don't be shy about building your own agents.

295
00:17:45,380 --> 00:17:48,010
 That's where the real-time savings live.

296
00:17:48,010 --> 00:17:49,720
 Thanks for watching.

